WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.670
The Living Planet Index follows 35,803
monitored populations of amphibians,

2
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.250
birds, fish, mammals and reptiles.

3
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:17.150
Between 1970 and 2022, those populations
fell by 73 per cent, on average.

4
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:21.130
In fresh water, the average
fall was 85 per cent.

5
00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:25.450
Yet about half of those populations
are stable or increasing.

6
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:27.630
So why should people care?

7
00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:30.050
Is it only for the animals' sake?

8
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.710
In this mission, you'll measure
how fast species are being lost,

9
00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.130
at home and worldwide, and
rank what drives the losses.

10
00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:41.850
Then you'll weigh the pressure
from 8.2 billion people,

11
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:47.450
the world's population in 2024,
and judge why ecosystems matter.

12
00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:52.430
Your mission: decide why falling
wildlife numbers should matter to people.
